  {
    "id": "elbourne2026_28",
    "source": {"bibkey": "elbourne-2026", "paperLabel": "(28)"},
    "reportedIn": null,
    "language": "stan1293",
    "primaryText": "Fido was cute.",
    "discourseSegments": [],
    "glossedTokens": [],
    "translation": "",
    "context": "",
    "judgment": "acceptable",
    "alternatives": [],
    "readings": [],
    "paperFeatures": [
      ["section", "3.2"]
    ],
    "comment": "The copula [BE PAST] takes the adjective without its optional E_R feature; the truth conditions (30) are that Fido was cute at some earlier time.",
    "verified": "page-image"
  },
  {
    "id": "elbourne2026_31",
    "source": {"bibkey": "elbourne-2026", "paperLabel": "(31)"},
    "reportedIn": null,
    "language": "stan1293",
    "primaryText": "Fido is a dog.",
    "discourseSegments": [],
    "glossedTokens": [],
    "translation": "",
    "context": "",
    "judgment": "acceptable",
    "alternatives": [],
    "readings": [],
    "paperFeatures": [
      ["section", "3.2"]
    ],
    "comment": "The indefinite article has a second meaning (32) that turns the noun into something of the adjective's type, so that the copula can take a dog as its argument; Montague's is of identity is the alternative.",
    "verified": "page-image"
  },
  {
    "id": "elbourne2026_36",
    "source": {"bibkey": "elbourne-2026", "paperLabel": "(36)"},
    "reportedIn": null,
    "language": "stan1293",
    "primaryText": "Joe is a former.",
    "discourseSegments": [],
    "glossedTokens": [],
    "translation": "",
    "context": "",
    "judgment": "ungrammatical",
    "alternatives": [],
    "readings": [],
    "paperFeatures": [
      ["section", "3.2"]
    ],
    "comment": "Predicted by the null-noun ENTITY analysis to mean that Joe is a former entity. Excluded here twice over: the article cannot combine with something of type ⟨eit,eit⟩, and former bears a compulsory E_R feature.",
    "verified": "page-image"
  },
  {
    "id": "elbourne2026_37",
    "source": {"bibkey": "elbourne-2026", "paperLabel": "(37)"},
    "reportedIn": null,
    "language": "stan1293",
    "primaryText": "Every red is attractive.",
    "discourseSegments": [],
    "glossedTokens": [],
    "translation": "",
    "context": "",
    "judgment": "ungrammatical",
    "alternatives": [],
    "readings": [],
    "paperFeatures": [
      ["section", "3.2"]
    ],
    "comment": "Predicted by the null-noun analysis to mean that every red individual is attractive. Excluded because every, of type ⟨eit,⟨eit,it⟩⟩, cannot combine with red, of type ⟨eit,eit⟩.",
    "verified": "page-image"
  },
  {
    "id": "elbourne2026_42",
    "source": {"bibkey": "elbourne-2026", "paperLabel": "(42)"},
    "reportedIn": null,
    "language": "stan1293",
    "primaryText": "The president is former.",
    "discourseSegments": [],
    "glossedTokens": [],
    "translation": "",
    "context": "",
    "judgment": "ungrammatical",
    "alternatives": [],
    "readings": [],
    "paperFeatures": [
      ["section", "3.2"]
    ],
    "comment": "The types work out, so the exclusion is syntactic: former, like mere, bears a compulsory E_R feature, and Argument Interpretability keeps the copula from selecting it.",
    "verified": "page-image"
  },
  {
    "id": "elbourne2026_43",
    "source": {"bibkey": "beesley-1982", "paperLabel": "p. 202"},
    "reportedIn": {"bibkey": "elbourne-2026", "paperLabel": "(43)"},
    "language": "stan1293",
    "primaryText": "Q: Which of the men over there is Quang? A: Quang is the short Vietnamese.",
    "discourseSegments": [
      "Which of the men over there is Quang?",
      "Quang is the short Vietnamese."
    ],
    "glossedTokens": [],
    "translation": "",
    "context": "",
    "judgment": "acceptable",
    "alternatives": [],
    "readings": [
      {"name": "short by the standard of men", "judgment": "acceptable"}
    ],
    "paperFeatures": [
      ["section", "3.3"]
    ],
    "comment": "Quang is said to be short for a man, not short for a Vietnamese person: the comparison class of a merely subsective adjective is supplied by the context, which either theory can do.",
    "verified": "page-image"
  },
  {
    "id": "elbourne2026_45",
